// lc_tank_vco_tuned_demo.clay (v0.0.59 — varactor frequency tuning verify)
//
// Builds on examples/lc_tank_vco_demo.clay (v0.0.57 — proved real
// transient oscillation at 200 MHz) and the v0.0.58 varactor primitive
// + with_varactor=True LC-VCO kwarg. This demo verifies that vctrl
// genuinely tunes the oscillation frequency through the varactor —
// not just amplitude / startup margin (the v0.0.57 contract).
//
// Topology:
//   Same cross-coupled NMOS LC-tank VCO as v0.0.57, plus two PMOS-MOSCAP
//   varactors symmetrically across the differential tank. Each varactor:
//     * gate = vctrl
//     * body (drain = source = bulk) = outp / outn (one each)
//   Varactor cap modulates with vctrl, adding to C_tank. f_osc varies
//   as 1/(2π·sqrt(L_tank·(C_tank + 2·C_var(vctrl)))).
//
// Sizing trade-off (v0.0.59 honest):
//   The v0.0.57 demo runs at 200 MHz with C_tank=63 pF (LC-resonant
//   for 200 MHz at L=10 nH). At that operating point, a 25 fF PMOS
//   varactor is only 0.04 % of the total tank cap — flat frequency
//   response. To make the varactor a meaningful fraction of the tank
//   cap, we drop C_tank to 100 fF (target frequency ~2 GHz) AND bump
//   M_var to 16 (parallel-finger multiplier on each PMOS varactor).
//   The resulting tuning ratio in sky130 BSIM4 is ≈ 5.4 % across
//   the workable vctrl swing.
//
//   * **Workable vctrl swing** is [0.9 V, 1.7 V] on sky130. Below 0.9 V
//     the tail NMOS Mtail (gate = vctrl) turns off and oscillation
//     dies. Above 1.7 V the varactor PMOS is in deep depletion and
//     the cap stops moving. M_tail=4 keeps the tail comfortably on
//     across the swing.
//
//   * **Tuning direction**: vctrl up → PMOS varactor goes from
//     inversion (large Cox cap) toward depletion (small cap) → total
//     C_tank goes down → f_osc goes up. Wait — actually the opposite
//     is true here: at low vctrl the PMOS gate-body voltage is in
//     deeper inversion → larger cap → lower f_osc. So vctrl=0.9 V
//     gives high cap → low f_osc; vctrl=1.7 V gives low cap → high
//     f_osc... no wait, that's also wrong. Empirically the measured
//     trend is vctrl up → freq down (because the PMOS varactor cap
//     increases with vctrl when vctrl approaches the body voltage at
//     vdd — opposite of what a simple Cgs cap suggests, but consistent
//     with the BSIM4 capmod=2.0 model used by sky130 1v8).
//     The .clay spec asserts only the magnitude of tuning, not the
//     sign, since the BSIM4 cap-vs-Vg curve depends on the model
//     parameters and is not the same as a textbook MOSCAP curve.
//
// What we measure:
//   The accompanying e2e test (`test_lc_tank_vco.py::
//   test_lc_tank_vco_tuned_demo_tunes_e2e`) runs three transient
//   simulations at vctrl = 0.9 V, 1.3 V, 1.7 V. Each sim extracts the
//   oscillation frequency via WHEN ... CROSS=N crossings. The test
//   asserts:
//     * freq(0.9V) ≠ freq(1.7V) by ≥ 5 % (the tuning contract)
//     * Monotonic direction across the three points
//
// Honest scope:
//   * Not run on gf180mcu in v0.0.59 — sky130 has the harder headroom
//     limit (1.8 V supply), so passing on sky130 is a stronger
//     contract.
//   * No phase noise / spurs verification.
//   * No corner sweep — tt @ 27 °C only.
//   * The frequency is in GHz range, not the PLL composer's expected
//     200 MHz. A future v0.0.60+ item is to revisit the PLL composer
//     so that a 2 GHz LC-VCO can drive the divider chain (or add a
//     prescaler between VCO and PLL feedback divider).

package examples.lc_tank_vco_tuned_demo;
pdk 'sky130';

cell LcTankVcoTuned(clk_out, vctrl, vdd, vss) {
  topology {
    style     = 'lc_tank_vco';
    rationale = 'LC-tank VCO with PMOS-MOSCAP varactor frequency tuning.';
  }

  // ─── v0.0.59 sizing overrides ──────────────────────────────────
  //   * with_varactor = 1 → enable the two PMOS-MOSCAP varactors
  //     across the differential tank.
  //   * C_tank = 200 fF → drops the tank from 63 pF (200 MHz target)
  //     to 200 fF (~2 GHz target), making the varactor cap a
  //     significant fraction of the total tank cap. (We tried 100 fF
  //     first — same 5.4 % tuning but ngspice convergence becomes
  //     fragile at vctrl=1.7 V; 200 fF is a safer operating point
  //     with identical measured tuning ratio.)
  //   * W_var = 80 µm, L_var = 0.5 µm, M_var = 16 → parallel-finger
  //     varactor with effective Wtot = 1280 µm of MOSCAP. The C-V
  //     curve at this size sweeps from ≈ 230 fF (deep inversion at
  //     vctrl=0V) to ≈ 410 fF (accumulation at vctrl=1.8V) per
  //     varactor — but only a fraction of that range is accessible
  //     within the workable vctrl swing.
  //   * W_osc = 40 µm, R_tank_series = 0.5 Ω → same as v0.0.57
  //     reliable-oscillation sizing.
  //   * M_tail = 4 → keeps the tail NMOS on at vctrl ≈ 0.9 V
  //     (lowest workable vctrl for the varactor tuning sweep).
  sizing {
    with_varactor = 1;
    C_tank        = 200e-15;
    W_osc         = 4e-5;
    R_tank_series = 0.5;
    W_var         = 8e-5;
    L_var         = 5e-7;
    M_var         = 16;
    M_tail        = 4;
  }

  // ─── Spec: oscillation health at vctrl ≈ middle of swing ───────
  // The .clay-level spec asserts only that the VCO oscillates with
  // reasonable amplitude. Frequency tuning verification lives in
  // the Python-side e2e test which runs three separate vctrl points
  // — that requires a parameterised sweep that the current
  // verify-clause spec grammar does not express (one .clay file →
  // one Testbench → one vctrl value).
  spec {
    clk_out_pp  : volt >= 0.5;
    clk_out_avg : volt >= 1.0;
    clk_out_avg : volt <= 2.5;
  }

  corners { tt; }
  temps   { 27C; }
}

verify LcTankVcoTuned;
